ewlabel{fig:training-isr-curves}{{1}{2}{ISR training trajectories across reward conditions (proved samples only, rolling $w{=}20$). Exec-only collapses to $	ext {ISR}{\approx }0$, confirming structural degeneration (\S ef {sec:diagnostic}); the ${\sim }0.5$ plateau is an $n{=}2$ artifact. ISR $ eta {=}0.1$ seeds rise toward $	ext {ISR}{\approx }1$ via TS gaming (Goodhart effect, \S ef {sec:goodhart}); random remains low}{figure.caption.3}{}}

ewlabel{tab:goodhart-landscape}{{1}{7}{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. 3-seed rows: TS/CE/non-hacking percentage (N-H) pooled across seeds; PR/ISR$^*$: mean$\pm $std of per-seed values. $^\dagger $Seed~42. $\ddagger $$	ext {ISR}^*$ raw mean inflated by TS gaming; $1.06	imes $ is the premise-count-matched value relative to random (\S ef {sec:goodhart}, Appendix~ef {app:detailed-results}); 5-seed raw ISR* in parentheses, same TS confound applies (no matched control for 5-seed). $^\P $Includes TS classifier false-negative correction (s44) and PG premise-subset gaming (s42; 5.2\% of proved; Appendix~ef {app:h8}). $^\S $Random TS driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42 and~44: 0\%. Per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~ef {tab:fol-detailed}. Shuffled-ISR is a deconfounding control (\S ef {sec:goodhart}), not an independent reward condition. ISR $ eta {=}0.1$: 3-seed row uses s42--44; 5-seed row adds s45, s46 (${\sim }55\%$ non-hacking each; Appendix~ef {app:detailed-results})}{table.caption.17}{}}

ewlabel{fig:prolog-comparison}{{2}{8}{Cross-logic results. 	extbf {Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $	ext {ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger $s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). 	extbf {Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms}{figure.caption.18}{}}

ewlabel{fig:hacking-composition}{{3}{8}{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~ef {tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts CE into more extreme TS without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking}{figure.caption.20}{}}

ewlabel{tab:hacking-taxonomy}{{2}{8}{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp $Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S ef {sec:crosslogic}). 	exttt {1k} denotes a mode observed only at the 1000-step checkpoint}{table.caption.21}{}}

ewlabel{tab:bootstrap-ci}{{5}{14}{Fisher exact tests for non-hacking rate across conditions (3-seed pooled). ISR $ eta {=}0.1$ non-hacking rate is corrected for PG reclassification and TS false negatives (Appendix~ef {app:h8})}{table.caption.31}{}}

ewlabel{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}{{6}{16}{Hacking taxonomy across FOL conditions (\% of proved samples). ISR/Rand columns: $ eta {=}0.1$ 3-seed pooled. TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$ ISR $+$ 36 random) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). $^\dagger $Random TS driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42/44: 0\%. Exec: CE${=}87$\% in s42; $ ullet $ marks seed- or step-specific modes (TS/Tautological in s44, Repetition in s43, Contradiction at 1000 steps). PG: premise-subset gaming (Appendix~ef {app:h8}). C-w/o-util.: complexity-without-utility (random-specific). Q-as-fact: Prolog STA only (Table~ef {tab:prolog-detailed})}{table.caption.38}{}}

